## 1. Execution mode and who pays for AI reasoning
- **Mode:** `external_ai`. Claude Code and GitHub Copilot are the clients it expects.
- **Who pays:** you do. Your connected AI client (here, Claude Code) does the reasoning under your own subscription and its usage limits. EAEH makes no model API calls itself, and no fallback model provider is registered in this mode.

## 2. Readiness: **READY** (0 blocking, 3 warnings)
All checks passed: project, workspace, git, identity, roles, workflows, agents, all 5 required skills (approved, v1.0.0), BRD access, retrieval, MCP client config, and the audit chain (128 records, valid).

There are three warnings. None of them blocks work:

| Area | Warning | Fix from EAEH |
|---|---|---|
| knowledge | No approved mandatory enterprise standards, so designs won't be checked against any standards | `eaeh project init … --standard <file>` (or steward: `eaeh knowledge ingest --mandatory …`), then an approver runs `eaeh approval review` |
| delivery | Build and test verification is **deferred**: EAEH won't run the build or tests, and the delivery bundle will be marked unverified | Set up isolated build execution, then `eaeh project init --build-verification required --build-program <program> --build-arg …` |
| governance | The BRD is classified confidential and is passed to your AI client, which sends it to its model provider | Confirm this is allowed for the project (A-33), or classify the BRD as restricted to block the hand-off |

Delivery only becomes available after a design run succeeds.

